# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 3 - Consequence Rule" and HoareLogic.pdf, section "Consequence rule".
id: assign-consequence-range
title: Weakening the precondition and strengthening the postcondition
topic: Skip and consequence
level: 2
source: Slides 3, Consequence Rule; HoareLogic.pdf, "Consequence rule"
tags: consequence, assignment

task:
  Prove the triple with the full consequence rule: weaken the
  precondition to $1 <= x <= 9$, and strengthen the postcondition to
  $10 <= y <= 90$.

pre: 2 <= x <= 8
post: 0 <= y <= 100

program:
  {P}
  {P'}
  y := x * 10
  {Q'}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Choose a stronger postcondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE
text:
  The program can only produce values of $y$ between $20$ and $80$, so the
  bounds $0$ and $100$ of the postcondition are far from what happens. We
  choose a simpler and stronger postcondition $Q'$, the multiples of ten
  from $10$ to $90$ for the values $x = 1, ..., 9$.
set: Q' = 10 <= y <= 90
vc: 10 <= y <= 90 ==> 0 <= y <= 100
why:
  Every number between $10$ and $90$ is between $0$ and $100$. This is the
  third premise of the consequence rule, $Q' ==> Q$.

step: Apply the assignment rule to the new postcondition
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: y := x * 10
calc:
  wp(y := x * 10, Q')
  = (10 <= y <= 90)[y <- x * 10]
  = 10 <= x * 10 <= 90
  = 1 <= x <= 9           // divide by 10
set: P' = 1 <= x <= 9
instance: ASSIGN
  ---
  {1 <= x <= 9} y := x * 10 {10 <= y <= 90}

step: Weaken the precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: 2 <= x <= 8 ==> 1 <= x <= 9
why:
  The interval $[2, 8]$ is included in $[1, 9]$. So $P$ is stronger than
  $P'$, which is the first premise of the consequence rule.

step: Apply the consequence rule
rule: CONSEQUENCE
instance: CONSEQUENCE
  2 <= x <= 8 ==> 1 <= x <= 9
  {1 <= x <= 9} y := x * 10 {10 <= y <= 90}
  10 <= y <= 90 ==> 0 <= y <= 100
  ---
  {2 <= x <= 8} y := x * 10 {0 <= y <= 100}
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The consequence rule lets us prove a triple through a different triple,
  as long as the precondition gets stronger and the postcondition gets
  weaker. The proof outline shows the two steps as the implications
  $\{P\}$ to $\{P'\}$ and $\{Q'\}$ to $\{Q\}$.
